      "id": 1,
      "text": "Overbrook Station on City Line near 63rd Street sparked the neighborhood’s residential boom in the late 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://collaborativehistory.gse.upenn.edu/stories/overbrook"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Pennsylvania Railroad’s Main Line trains turned the area into an affluent suburb northwest of Philadelphia.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://collaborativehistory.gse.upenn.edu/stories/overbrook"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Overbrook Farms, a Georgian Revival enclave near Drexel Road, showcases the area’s early 20th‑century residential architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://collaborativehistory.gse.upenn.edu/stories/overbrook"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mill Creek, joined by George’s Run, supplied water to textile factories and later became a conduit for their waste.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://waterhistoryphl.org/articles/overbrook-as-it-was/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Before European settlers, the Lenape used Overbrook as a hunting ground; by the 18th century it was a fertile farm and mill district.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://collaborativehistory.gse.upenn.edu/stories/overbrook"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "During the 19th‑century industrial boom, textile factories in Overbrook employed hundreds and, by mid‑century, switched to steam while still relying on creeks for water and waste.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
